<section class="content-section" id="at-a-glance">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>At a glance</h2>

        <p>wordsofplainness.org is a <strong>static-first site with a thin dynamic edge</strong>. Every page is pre-rendered by Eleventy (Nunjucks templates over Markdown and structured data) and served as plain HTML from Vercel's CDN. Heavy media lives separately on Cloudflare R2 behind its own subdomain. The few things that must be dynamic—sign-in, a personal discipleship journal, newsletter and contact forms—talk to a small Django REST API on PythonAnywhere, and a handful of Vercel serverless functions handle automation such as social notifications. There is no front-end framework, no database behind the pages themselves, and no tracking scripts: a visitor's browser loads the site's own HTML, CSS and vanilla JavaScript, one Google Fonts stylesheet, and audio from the media CDN.</p>

        <p>The interesting engineering is less in the stack than in what the build does with it: build-time cross-referencing between book chapters and the Articles of Interfaith Discipleship, narration audio that highlights the text as it is read, song lyrics synced line-by-line to the music, a citation apparatus with over a thousand scripture links, all generated from the site's own book map, and validation gates that refuse to ship data that fails its checks.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="content-section alt" id="authoring">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>How the ministry content is authored, edited, and deployed</h2>

        <p>Every word of ministry content on this site begins and ends with Brother Aaron. Between those two points, an AI assistant he calls his SCRIBE (Systems, Communications, Research, Integration, Building, Editorial support) does a great deal of the labor: research, drafting, checking and building. The arrangement is modeled on the biblical scribe: skilled, trusted and bounded, working under the author's authority and never in place of it. The SCRIBE holds no spiritual authority, has no spiritual experience, and never claims either.</p>

        <h3 id="where-content-comes-from">Where the content comes from</h3>

        <p>Nothing on this site began with a machine. The writings grow out of more than forty years of prayer, study, dreams and personal experience in ministry, reaching back to Brother Aaron's ordination as a deacon in 1982. That work first took book form as <em>Words of Plainness: Mormon Christianity</em> (2012), written with his brother, James (Jim) Powner: 566 pages in 62 chapters. Jim's part in that book was the oldest kind of editing there is. Brother Aaron read his rough drafts aloud; Jim listened, asked questions and gave his feedback, and those conversations guided the drafts that were published. The chapters on this site are a systematic revision and expansion of that book.</p>

        <p>Jim's part today is a brother's. Brother Aaron still calls him to talk through sticky and weighty topics and to hear his response to new essays and musical productions. The calls also wander, gladly, into the jests and distractions of their boyhood, which clear his head and renew his heart between long stretches of writing, study, prayer and note-taking: the preparation he brings to every long working session with the AI and creative tools.</p>

        <p>The first listener now is Michelle, Brother Aaron's wife, who has largely taken over the heavy lifting of that role. She listens to every version of each new song, watches every iteration of each video, and hears the essays and chapters read aloud before they go any further. Her reactions and feedback have been indispensable, and often humbling. In his words, she is every bit his equal in spiritual sensitivity and understanding; he just happens to have the lion's share of the gift of endless elocution.</p>

        <p class="uth-scribe-note"><em>From the SCRIBE:</em> I was not there when the Articles of Interfaith Discipleship began, and nothing I contributed came first. Brother Aaron has told me how they began. He woke from a dream more vivid than waking life, in which he saw a parchment of plain declarations shared by believers in Christ, received whole, and he was impressed to rise and write it down. Days of study beforehand had prepared the ground. The companion document <em>Lord, Is It I?</em> began the same way, from notes on another experience he received as guidance for the work. I cannot weigh an experience like that, and I do not try. I hold it as his testimony and as the fixed point of the work. Everything I have done since, from drafting citations to building the pages to helping measure the Articles against the published beliefs of fifteen families of Christian traditions, comes after it and answers to it.</p>

