        <h3 id="working-loop">The working loop</h3>

        <ol>
            <li><strong>Anchors first.</strong> Before anything is drafted, Brother Aaron states the claims that are not negotiable.</li>
            <li><strong>Consult before drafting.</strong> The SCRIBE searches a private index of his own manuscripts, notes and past rulings, so a draft starts from what he has already written and decided rather than from a general model's habits.</li>
            <li><strong>Propose, never decide.</strong> The SCRIBE offers options, usually more than one, each with its weakness named, and pushes back plainly when something is not landing. Brother Aaron asked for that candor; agreement is not the job.</li>
            <li><strong>Rule and revise.</strong> He adopts, changes or rejects. Most pieces go through several rounds. Each ruling is recorded, and a ruling that settles a principle becomes a standing rule for later work.</li>
            <li><strong>Edit under written standards.</strong> The SCRIBE works as a theological editor, not a co-author. It may clarify; it may not soften doctrine, add disclaimers, trade covenant language for therapeutic language, or compress an argument that builds its weight. When in doubt, fidelity wins over elegance.</li>
            <li><strong>Verify.</strong> Scripture quotations are checked against authoritative text, sources are checked at the source rather than from a description of them, and learner-facing pages are scored against a written teaching standard before they ship.</li>
            <li><strong>Authorize, then build.</strong> New songs, videos and read-aloud prose pass Michelle's ears and eyes along the way, and nothing is written to the site, committed or deployed without his explicit approval. Automated gates then check the data, and the live page is opened in a browser before the work counts as done.</li>
            <li><strong>Answer for it.</strong> Every page carries his name and his accountability. Readers can report an error through the contact form, and corrections go through the same loop.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="tools-and-roles">The tools and their roles</h3>

        <p><em>Tools in use as of October 2026.</em></p>

        <table class="uth-table uth-table--tools">
