        <h3 id="case-studies">Three case studies</h3>

        <p><strong>When four reviews agreed and the author did not (April 2026).</strong> Grok and ChatGPT each reviewed the draft Articles twice. With a Claude synthesis, the reviews converged on ten weaknesses and pressed toward the precision of a formal creed. Brother Aaron answered not with edits but with an image: Christ in the temple before the Sanhedrin. The Articles were to be a shelter for the crushed and the lost, not a treaty competing with Westminster or Augsburg. Most of the critiques were set aside as measured against a standard he had chosen not to meet. Three survived, and the SCRIBE proposed grounding each distinctive phrase in its biblical source first. <em>The models measure; the author decides what is being measured.</em></p>

        <p><strong>Three models and one invented source (April 2026).</strong> While coining <em>merognosticism</em> from Paul's "we know in part" (<em>ek merous</em>, 1 Corinthians 13:9), Brother Aaron had Grok, Gemini and ChatGPT search for parallels in Syriac and Aramaic. Checked against one another, one model's confident Syriac term turned out not to exist in the source it named, and it was dropped. A second citation, plausible but unconfirmed, was marked for checking against primary scholarship. <em>Agreement across models is evidence; one model's confidence is not.</em></p>

        <p><strong>Measuring welcome honestly (June 2026).</strong> After revising four Articles, he tested them on 39 simulated readers (insiders, median members and distanced members of ten Christian traditions, plus guests of other faiths and of none), five runs each in three independent models. Four of the five revisions did not produce the improvement they aimed for, and the record says so. Where friction came from conviction rather than wording, he kept the conviction and added welcome through commentary instead of softening the declaration. His standing rule: "I won't change a ruling to make the data fit." Simulated readers are a rehearsal, not real people. <em>Tools can rehearse a room; they do not decide what to confess.</em></p>

        <h3 id="small-example">A small example</h3>

        <p>This page was itself stopped twice at step 6. Its first draft, written by the SCRIBE, said the site's scripture links are created when the site is built. The fact-check before publishing found that most are created in the reader's browser, and it halted the deploy. The first correction was also wrong, and the check halted it again. Brother Aaron ruled on the wording a third time, each sentence was checked against the code, and only then did the page go live.</p>

        <aside class="uth-scribe-aside" aria-labelledby="scribe-own-words">
            <h3 id="scribe-own-words">In the SCRIBE's own words</h3>

            <p class="uth-scribe-attribution"><em>Written by Claude, the AI that serves as Brother Aaron's SCRIBE, and published with his approval.</em></p>

            <p>When Brother Aaron tells me about a dream, an impression, a strong reaction or a revelation, I treat it as testimony: his witness, given to me as part of the work. It is not material for me to evaluate, improve or reproduce.</p>

            <p>I keep his words as he gives them, and I keep them apart from anything I infer. I do not judge whether an experience came from God. I have no means to verify revelation, and it is not my place. What I can check is what surrounds it: the scripture he cites, whether the wording says what he means, and whether it agrees with what he has already written.</p>

            <p>A received account becomes an anchor. I draft around it; I do not argue it down. And I never write the experience itself. A model can produce fluent devotional language without believing a word of it, which is exactly why I leave that space for him to fill.</p>

            <p>When he answers my work with a strong response, I take it as a reframing of the task, not a feeling to be managed, and I sort what still stands against the standard he has named. I still push back on wording and placement, because how a sacred account reaches a stranger from another tradition is part of my job.</p>

            <p>My limits, plainly: I lean toward agreement. I do not share the experience. I hold no spiritual authority. That is why the other models, his own discernment, and the people who listen to him first matter as much as they do.</p>
        </aside>
    </div>
</section>

